{
  "chapter": "reliability/backpressure",
  "tier": "drill",
  "notes": [
    "Admission control decides what gets attempted at all. The unit is the whole run,",
    "estimated in input tokens, because a run that starts and does not finish has spent",
    "real money and produced nothing.",
    "The client limiter sits below the provider's quota, so load is shed deliberately and a",
    "429 becomes an alert that your model of your own quota is wrong.",
    "Each priority gets a reserved share of that effective quota, so a batch job cannot take",
    "the interactive floor. Each tenant gets a cap, so one customer's burst cannot take the",
    "shared quota.",
    "Retries are generated inside runs that were already admitted, so they bypass the door",
    "unless they are counted against the same budget. They are counted here.",
    "effectiveConcurrentRuns is the number that belongs on the wall: the input-token budget",
    "over what one run consumes per minute. It is usually single digits, and the worker",
    "pool is not."
  ],
  "config": {
    "inputTpm": 2000000,
    "clientLimitBps": 8500,
    "shareBps": { "interactive": 7000, "background": 2000, "batch": 1000 },
    "tenantCapBps": 2500,
    "backoffMs": { "interactive": 5000, "background": 30000, "batch": 120000 },
    "tenantBackoffMs": 30000,
    "profile": { "avgContextTokens": 30000, "turnsPerMinute": 15 }
  },
  "cases": [
